# Seven defences behind the goodbye bow, seven mutations, every one of them red.
# Run with scratchpad/mutate_ts.py: one exact string swap at a time, the pattern
# asserted to hit exactly once, the file restored after.
#   G1, G2, G3   npx vitest run src/components/chat/avatarBow.test.ts
#   G4           npx vitest run src/components/chat/ChatWidget.test.tsx
#   G5, G6, G7   npx vitest run src/components/chat/
#
# The last three run against the whole directory, so their "one red" is an
# exclusivity across every test in src/components/chat/. The first four are
# scoped to one file, so theirs is an exclusivity within that file.
#
# They land on FIVE distinct tests, not seven, and the mapping is the point:
#
#   G1, G2   both redden BOTH bow assertions. `facingSign` returning one sign
#            and `bow` dropping the term it multiplies by are two points on one
#            chain, so neither can be wrong on its own.
#   G6, G7   each redden ONE of that pair and only that one. They exist to show
#            the head half and the spine half are separately covered, which is
#            exactly what G1 and G2 cannot show.
#   G3       leanBack, all three families. Nothing else touches it. The swap
#            negates leanBack's curve outright rather than applying facingSign
#            to it, which is why all three families redden; a facingSign-shaped
#            edit would have reddened only vroid-sample-b, because `fwd` is +1
#            on the two 1.0 bodies. The stronger swap is the one that ran.
#   G4       the goodbye. Nothing else touches it.
#   G5       the loop's version read. Nothing else touches it.
#
# Three of the seven owe their redness to a reviewer reading the first draft:
# G6 and G7 did not exist at all, and G5 existed but was GREEN.
#
# G5 was GREEN in the first pass, under a paragraph explaining why the seam
# could not be closed without a test that boots the engine against a real body.
# That was wrong: avatarGuideEngine.wiring.test.ts already closes the same seam
# for pinArms by reading the engine's SOURCE -- no VRM, no WebGL, and the whole
# file inside bow-0910-timing.log's 2ms-to-9ms band over nineteen runs -- and the
# gesture loop now carries the same three-line assertion. The first draft would
# have shipped an untested line under a paragraph arguing it had to stay
# untested.
#
# G6 and G7 exist because the first version of avatarBow.test.ts could not see
# HALF the fix. It read the HEAD bone's own world position, and rotating a bone
# does not move its own origin, so every millimetre it reported came from `sx`
# on the spine: dropping the version term from `hp` alone left the whole suite
# green. The head term now has its own assertion, which reads the EYE (about
# 57mm above the head pivot, so it does swing), and G6/G7 mutate the two halves
# separately to show neither is riding on the other.

